<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- HealthKit. This is not new capability, it is the entitlement the existing code has always
	     needed and never had: HealthDataService constructs HKHealthStore and calls
	     RequestAuthorizationToShare, and Info.plist carries NSHealthShareUsageDescription, but
	     without this key the authorisation callback simply comes back unsuccessful. The failure
	     mode is the quiet kind - no crash, no error dialog, just sleep data that never arrives -
	     which is why it survived this long.

	     Two things this obliges, both outside the repo:
	     - The App ID must have the HealthKit capability enabled, or signing fails. CI cannot catch
	       it: build-ios.yml publishes with CodesignKey="-".
	     - App Review asks what health data is read and why. The honest answer is short: sleep
	       analysis, read-only, to prefill the sleep fields of a diary entry. The share set passed
	       to RequestAuthorizationToShare is empty on purpose - the app never writes to Health -
	       and there is deliberately no NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription to match.

	     The cheaper alternative, if Health is not worth that on v1: drop this key, drop
	     NSHealthShareUsageDescription and both localised copies, and let the iOS branch of
	     HealthDataService report unsupported the way the Android branch already does. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Removed for v1: com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit.
	     It was added for the 1.1 GB generative model, and OnDeviceAi.GenerationOffered has been
	     false for everyone since 2026-08-10. What is left is the 123 MB encoder, which does not go
	     near the jetsam ceiling that entitlement was raising. An entitlement kept "just in case"
	     is not free: it has to be enabled on the App ID too, and a key in the binary that the
	     provisioning profile does not carry is a signing failure rather than a warning.
	     Restoring it is this, verbatim, in the same commit that flips GenerationOffered back:
	         <key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	         <true/>
	     It only ever took effect when the installing profile happened to carry it, so it bought
	     several hundred megabytes when it survived and nothing when it did not. -->
</dict>
</plist>
